<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 742–<a href="/years/809">809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ludger, Liudger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludger was a Frisian missionary educated at Utrecht and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> who preached among the Frisians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. Around 800 he founded a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werden">Werden</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, which became an imperial abbey ruling a small territory for a thousand years. He was also the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Münster and is venerated as a saint, buried at Werden.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludger.jpg" alt="Ludger" width="273" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saintliudger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludger's parents, Thiadgrim and Liafburg, were wealthy Christian Frisians of noble descent. In 753 he saw Boniface, the Apostle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, whose martyrdom soon after left a deep impression on him. He studied at the cathedral school of Utrecht under Gregory of Utrecht, went with Alubert to York in 767, was ordained deacon there and studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a>, with whom he formed a lifelong friendship. He left York in 772 when friction arose between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> and Frisians, and after Gregory's death in 775 he wrote his biography, the Vita Gregorii.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ordained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> on 7 July 777, he was given the missions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>, with Dokkum, where Boniface had died, as their centre, returning each autumn to teach at Utrecht. In 784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> persuaded the Frisians to drive out the missionaries and burn the churches. Ludger went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 785, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-i">Pope Adrian I</a> received him, and then lived by the Rule of Saint Benedict at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> without taking vows. After Widukind's submission and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s visit to Monte Cassino in 787 he returned to preach around the estuaries of the Hunze, Fivel and Ems, using his knowledge of the people's language, and on Heligoland he built a church and turned a well sacred to the old gods into a font.</p><p>In 793 he declined the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> but offered to evangelise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was added to his field. At Mimigernaford he built a house of canons living by the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrodegang-of-metz">Chrodegang of Metz</a>, from which the place took the name Münster. He built churches at Billerbeck, Coesfeld, Nottuln and elsewhere, and a house at Nottuln for his sister Gerburgis that became the first convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. Archbishop Hildebold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> consecrated him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> on 30 March 805.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On Passion Sunday 809 he preached at Coesfeld and Billerbeck and died that evening at the age of sixty-six. Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werden">Werden</a> disputed his remains; his brother Hildegrim, after consulting the emperor, decided for Werden. Lives of him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altfrid">Altfrid</a>'s contemporary work to a book of miracles of about 1170 show his legend growing. He is patron saint of Werden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a>, and is shown as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> with a church and book or between two geese.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saint Ludger</b>, a Frisian missionary and later first bishop of Münster, founded a monastery at Werden on the Ruhr around 799 on land he had acquired.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-werden">Imperial Abbey of Werden</a></li><li><blockquote>The Frisian missionary <b>Liudger</b> founded a monastery at Mimigernaford around 793 and became the first bishop of Münster in 805.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-munster">Prince-Bishopric of Münster</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zuilen, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Billerbeck, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.11,5.09&amp;year=785" aria-label="Zuilen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4665 363 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ludger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5115 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5115 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.11,5.09&amp;year=785">What was here in 785 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
